# These files are read by a shell inside the Linux distro, never by Windows.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is normal on a Windows checkout, and it silently breaks every one of them:
# `dash` reads `set -e\r` as `set: Illegal option -` and the script dies on line 3, naming neither
# the file nor the reason. It cost an hour in the Flutter pack, where a scaffold created its project
# and then quietly skipped the two steps that made the project buildable.
#
# They are LF in this checkout today, which is luck rather than policy: nothing has re-cloned this
# repo on a machine with autocrlf on. `eol=lf` makes it policy.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
